\section{Trade-offs in tool usage}
\label{sub:trade-off}

Leveraging tools often brings better performance, however, should we always use tools? More concretely, is the performance gain from using tools worthy of the c
omputation cost spent for LMs to learn to use tools, or the inference cost at test time?
Existing works mainly focus on task accuracy, but a more nuanced picture emerges when we take other factors into account.
We empirically study the performance gain and learning cost of various methods on their experimented datasets in \autoref{tab:api-benchmarks}, using which we di
scover more efficient (i.e., achieve greater gains with less compute) methods and tasks that benefit more from tools.

